    <summary>Let AI agents see and edit maps in Valve Hammer Editor - live.</summary>
    <description>Ask your agent *"put a warm light at each corner of the room and name them by the scheme"* - and watch the entities appear in the Hammer you already have open. No plugins, no patched editor: Hammer-MCP is an [MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) server that talks to Valve Hammer Editor through the shell channel Valve built into the editor (`Worldcraft_ShellMessageWnd`, the mechanism behind `editor_sendcommand.cpp` in the Source SDK) and through Hammer's own menu commands.

![Hammer-MCP](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/EpicMorg/Hammer-MCP/v0.6.0/docs/media/screenshot-2.png)

## What works
- Create point entities with keyvalues, set keyvalues, rotate and delete entities - live, with Hammer's undo (Ctrl+Z works)
- Save / undo / redo / Run Map / Check for Problems through Hammer's menu commands
- Map knowledge from the saved `.vmf`: summary, entities, outputs and the I/O graph, validation against the game's FGD, asset checks
- Compile with Hammer's own Run Map sequences, parsed compile log, leak trace, compiled `.bsp` entities
- Recognises the running Hammer build by hash; token auth, localhost by default (LAN-capable)

## Supported editors
Stock Source Hammer (Source SDK 2013, HL2, Portal, TF2, CS:S, L4D2, Portal 2, GMod, ...; x86 and x64), [Hammer++](https://github.com/ficool2/HammerPlusPlus-Website) 8867+, Slammin' Hammer, Black Mesa; menu commands and map files for Strata Hammer; read-only Source 2 (`.vmap`) and GoldSrc maps.

## Quick start
1. Run **Hammer-MCP** (Start menu or `hammer-mcp`) - it lives in the tray.
2. Pick the Hammer process -&gt; **Attach** -&gt; **Start**.
3. Connect your agent (tray menu -&gt; *Copy client config*):
   `claude mcp add --transport http hammer http://127.0.0.1:47800/mcp`
4. Open a map in Hammer and save it. Ask away.

Without the tray app, MCP clients that start servers themselves can run `hammer-mcp-cli` (`--stdio` by default, `--http`, `--auto` / `--pid N`, `--mode`).

## Safety and VAC
Hammer-MCP never injects into or writes to game processes: the shell channel and menu commands are window messages to the standalone editor process, so it is safe to run next to games with Valve Anti-Cheat.

## Package notes
- Portable: the official release zip is extracted into the package folder; `hammer-mcp` (tray app, GUI) and `hammer-mcp-cli` (console) are put on the PATH, and a Start menu shortcut is created.
- Windows 10/11, x86 and x64 (the 32-bit build also works with a 64-bit Hammer). Settings are kept in `%APPDATA%\EpicMorg\Hammer-MCP`.
- Status: beta.
</description>
    <releaseNotes>## [0.6.0] - 2026-11-14

The MCP server without the tray app (`hammer-mcp-cli`, stdio / HTTP) and a container image for MCP catalogs.

### Added
- **`hammer-mcp-cli`**: the MCP server without the tray app - stdio (default) or HTTP, Hammer picked with `--auto` / `--pid`, `--mode`; shipped in the release zip. A `Dockerfile` runs it in a container (it starts and lists its tools without a Hammer - what MCP catalogs such as Glama check).

### Changed
- Public repository: status **beta**, `SECURITY.md` (private vulnerability reports), `CONTRIBUTING.md` (how to report an unknown Hammer build), issue templates, demo GIF / video and screenshots in the README, articles on the Valve Developer Community ([EN](https://developer.valvesoftware.com/wiki/Hammer_MCP_Server), [RU](https://developer.valvesoftware.com/wiki/Ru/Hammer_MCP_Server)).
- About: fixed the link to epicm.org.

Full changelog: https://github.com/EpicMorg/Hammer-MCP/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md

